<section class="band" id="why">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="band__head" data-reveal>
      <h2>A hand is not a mouse</h2>
      <p class="lead">Most attempts at spatial control map your hand to the pointer and stop there: a few awkward gestures, laid on top of an interface built for a keyboard and mouse. HoloTouch starts from what hands already do.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="goals" data-reveal="children">
      <article class="card goal">
        <div class="goal__hand" data-hand="index"></div>
        <h3>Intuitive</h3>
        <p>Pinch to pick a window up. Make a fist to shut it. Turn a knob in the air for the volume. Gestures your hands already know.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card goal">
        <div class="goal__hand" data-hand="two" data-side="left"></div>
        <h3>Space first</h3>
        <p>It uses the room around you. Tip two fingers at the screen to scroll, or touch a fist to your chin and every window is laid out as a card.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card goal">
        <div class="goal__hand" data-hand="ily"></div>
        <h3>Extensible</h3>
        <p>Replace what the pie menu holds, add macros you fingerspell, and record your own hands to teach it your alphabet.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card goal">
        <div class="goal__hand" data-hand="aim" data-side="left"></div>
        <h3>Quick and consistent</h3>
        <p>Thirty frames a second on a laptop CPU, with an ordinary webcam. A slow hand does not jitter and a fast one does not lag.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="aside" data-reveal>The closest you will get to Tony Stark tossing windows around.</p>
  </div>
</section>
